Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/8] ionic: devmem TCP receive, against an emulated device

This is a proof of concept, not a submission. It lets a dma-buf be
bound to an ionic Rx queue (NETDEV_CMD_BIND_RX) and received into with
recvmsg(MSG_SOCK_DEVMEM), on a device that splits headers from
payload. No ionic firmware does that. The device here is an emulator
(rocm-ernic) that implements an interface invented for the purpose, and
patch 2 holds its definitions: placeholders, clearly marked, which must
not go upstream. What the series shows is the Linux side, and the shape
of the interface a device would need.

The feature bit in particular, BIT_ULL(17) of the Rx queue features,
is simply the next one free in ionic_if.h. It was not checked against
what shipped firmware reports there, and a device that already uses it
would be taken for one that splits headers.

It sits on top of the netmem Tx RFC series ("ionic: support netmem
TX"), on v7.4-rc3.

Patch 1 makes ionic an "ops locked" driver: the ndos and ethtool ops
run under the netdev instance lock, and the work items and recovery
paths that bring queues up or down take it. It does not depend on
anything else here and is the part that most needs review and a run
under lockdep.

Patch 2 has the placeholder definitions.

Patch 3 uses CHECKSUM_UNNECESSARY on header split queues, where
CHECKSUM_COMPLETE can't be relied on because the payload may be
unreadable. Other queues are unchanged.

Patch 4 converts the Rx buffer bookkeeping to netmem. No functional
change, and independent of the device interface as well.

Patch 5 is the header split datapath: a header page pool next to the
payload pool, headers copied to the linear area, payload only in the
frags and never looked at.

Patch 6 is the tcp-data-split ring parameter, and the exclusions (XDP,
Rx timestamps on all packets, rx-push).

Patch 7 keeps an Rx queue receiving when it can't get buffers for the
whole ring, and looking for buffers when it has none. That is rare with
pages and ordinary with a dma-buf that is smaller than the ring.

Patch 8 adds the queue management ops and lets the payload pool take a
memory provider. The queue ops restart every queue rather than one;
see the patch for why, and for what real firmware would have to
answer. The dma-buf should cover the ring: one page per descriptor at
an MTU of 1500, more above a page, plus what the application holds.

What is missing for the upstream selftests: ionic has no n-tuple flow
steering and no RSS contexts, so nothing can steer a flow to the bound
queue. ncdevmem has to be run with -n, on one queue or with a source
port chosen for the emulator's flow hash.

What only a device vendor can say:

 - whether header split can exist, how it is negotiated, and where the
   header length is reported;
 - whether one Rx queue can be reinitialised while the LIF is up, and
   whether Q_CONTROL(DISABLE) is synchronous. For devmem a late write
   lands in memory already given back to user space;
 - which packets are split (this series assumes TCP over IPv4 or IPv6
   without extension headers, and nothing with a bad checksum or
   padding), and what the checksum flags mean for tunnels;
 - whether a split queue can also timestamp, or live in the CMB.

Testing: x86_64, gcc 13, W=1 and sparse, at every patch. The whole
series was run in two KVM guests (v7.4-rc3 with KASAN and
PROVE_LOCKING, ionic_rdma loaded) on an emulated ionic that implements
the placeholder interface, and with that interface switched off:

 - ifup/ifdown, ring, channel (shared and split interrupts) and MTU
   changes, XDP attach and detach, the other ethtool ops, a PCI
   function reset with the netdev up and down, and module unload, idle
   and under traffic;
 - tcp-data-split with ordinary pages: TCP over IPv4, IPv6 and a VLAN,
   UDP and fragments, with the payload checked at the receiver;
 - devmem Rx with ncdevmem -n -L -v, over IPv4, IPv6 and a VLAN, on
   one queue and on one of two, with shared and with split interrupts,
   from a plain sender and from a devmem Tx sender, and with the
   binding in place: killing ncdevmem, ifdown/ifup, a ring resize, a
   PCI function reset and module unload;
 - devmem Rx with a ring larger than the dma-buf, at an MTU of 1500
   and of 9000, and with two queues on one dma-buf;
 - bind and unbind in a loop while ring, channel and MTU changes,
   ifdown/ifup and PCI function resets ran next to it.

With knobs added to the driver for the purpose, which are not part of
the series, also: ndo_queue_start() failing once and twice, at bind and
at unbind; ionic_lif_handle_fw_down() and ionic_lif_handle_fw_up(),
idle, under traffic, with a binding, and starting in the middle of a
bind and of an unbind; the Tx timeout work; and the link going down and
up in the link check. The firmware was alive throughout, so none of
this saw a command time out.

lockdep stayed quiet. KASAN reported nothing in the code the series
adds. It did report a use-after-free in page_pool_nl_fill(), from a
bug that is in ionic today: ionic_reconfigure_queues() leaks the
temporary queues, with their page pools, when the queue count grows
("ip link set down; ethtool -L 2; ip link set up; ethtool -L 4"), and a
leaked pool points at the NAPI of a queue that was freed. The change
to ionic_swap_queues() in patch 8 happens to take that pointer away,
and the report with it. The leak is still there; its fix is a patch
of its own and not in this series.

Every bind logs a DMA-API warning, "mapping sg segment longer than
device claims to support", when the core maps the dma-buf: ionic sets
no maximum DMA segment size. Not addressed here.

Not tested:

 - real hardware;
 - a firmware that really stops: the heartbeat path, commands timing
   out during recovery, and a link flap reported by the device;
 - padded frames, frames whose headers overflow the header buffer,
   IPv4 options and IPv6 extension headers arriving at the driver. The
   emulator's unit tests cover what it does with them; nothing sent
   them to a guest;
 - a dma-buf pool that the application drains by holding its tokens;
 - Rx timestamps and CMB rings.

Stephen Bates (8):
  ionic: run the netdev ops under the instance lock
  ionic: add placeholder definitions for Rx header split
  ionic: trust the device's L4 checksum on header split queues
  ionic: track Rx buffers as netmem
  ionic: put headers in the linear area on header split queues
  ionic: support the tcp-data-split ring parameter
  ionic: keep an Rx queue going when its buffers run out
  ionic: add queue management ops and allow unreadable Rx payload

